8.2 Hysteresis Settings


Hysteresis, also known as magnetic lag, is a common term in the automation industry,
typically used to describe a phenomenon in sensors when detecting objects or measuring
physical changes. Hysteresis can be defined as the error amount between the applied
control signal and the system’s resulting changes. In KTM5900, hysteresis is placed at the
filter output position to prevent extremely minor signal interferences from causing
high-order changes in the angle signal.


In KTM5900, fixed offset hysteresis is provided. When the direction changes, the lag
module will offset the input angle with a preset hysteresis value. This means that when
using this type of hysteresis, there will be a fixed angle offset in a certain direction
(clockwise or counterclockwise). The figure below shows an example of input and output

