## 12 Alarms and Detection (IRQ)


MSB LSB

0x120 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 IRQ.OD0


0x123 7 6 5 4 IRQ.SIE[7:0] 3 2 1 0


0x124 7 6 5 4 IRQ.SIE[15:8] 3 2 1 0


0x129 7 6 5 4 IRQ.STA[7:0] 3 2 1 0


0x12a 7 6 5 4 IRQ.STA[15:8] 3 2 1 0


Table 20: Alarm and Detection Registers (Section IRQ)


Irq_od


ctrREG IRQ.OD


Interrupt Pin Configuration


ctrREG

IRQ.OD determines the output method of the interrupt pin. When set to 0, the interrupt
pin is a push-pull output, suitable for scenarios requiring strong driving capability. When
set to 1, the interrupt pin is an open-drain output, suitable for scenarios where multiple
outputs need to be connected on the same line, such as sharing interrupt signals among
multiple devices.


Stat_latch


ctrREG IRQ.STA [15:0]


Alarm Status


ctrREG

In the IRQ module, the IRQ.STA register plays a crucial role in latching and clearing

specific alarm states. This register contains multiple bits, each representing a specific state
or error in the system. Here are detailed explanations of these bits:


AMR Input Sine/Cosine Amplitude Anomaly



ctrREG IRQ.STA [2]=



SIG

alampAmp



When the amplitude of the sine or cosine signal input from the AMR sensor is too low or
too high, this bit will be set. This may be due to sensor damage, signal path interference, or
configuration errors.


tmrMisMatch - TMR Input Mismatch


ctrREG IRQ.STA [6]: Indicates that the TMR0 and TMR1 timer inputs are inconsistent with the

sine/cosine signal input from the AMR sensor, possibly due to signal asynchrony, clock
errors, or other hardware issues.


filterDelay - Excessive Filter Lag


ctrREG IRQ.STA [7]: This bit will be set when the response lag of the filter exceeds the set normal

range, usually indicating improper filter configuration or insufficient processing capacity.


abzLimit - ABZ Output Limit


ctrREG IRQ.STA [8]: If the output speed of the ABZ encoder exceeds the system’s processing


40 of 43


KTM5900



capacity or preset safety limits ( ctrREG A.ABL ), this bit will be set, usually indicating system

performance issues or potential hardware failure.


abzFatal - Severe ABZ Output Lag


ctrREG IRQ.STA [9]: When the lag of the ABZ encoder output exceeds 180 degrees, this bit is set,

indicating that position data may be inaccurate or lost, usually due to serious faults in the
encoder or driving circuit.


crcFail - SPI CRC Decoding Error


ctrREG IRQ.STA [10]: The setting of this bit indicates an error in the CRC check of data received

via SPI communication, possibly due to transmission errors, signal interference, or hardware
failures.


mtp1bit - MTP Single Bit Error Detected


ctrREG IRQ.STA [11]: This bit is set when a 1-bit error is detected by the MTP (Multi-Times

Programmable Memory), possibly due to memory damage, programming errors, or
hardware faults.


uvderVol - Undervoltage Detected


ctrREG IRQ.STA [12]: This bit is set when a voltage lower than the normal operating range is

detected, possibly due to unstable power supply, power adapter failure, or system circuit
issues.


overVol - Overvoltage Detected


ctrREG IRQ.STA [13]: When the system detects a voltage exceeding the safe operating range, this

bit is set. Excessive voltage can cause system damage or unstable operation and usually
requires immediate inspection of the power supply and circuit.


Stat_ie


ctrREG IRQ.SIE [15:0]


Status Interrupt Enable Configuration


ctrREG

IRQ.SIE allows the system to enable or disable the IRQ PIN foot output alarm function.



ctrREG



ctrREG



Corresponds bit by bit with IRQ.STA . For example, if bit13 of IRQ.SIE is set to 1, an alarm

will be output by IRQ when overvoltage occurs. If bit13 is 0, no alarm will be output by IRQ
for overvoltage.



Corresponds bit by bit with



